 main=f'''<header><p class="eyebrow">En Rêve / Phase 6</p><h1>Less initial date-picker DOM, measured first-use cost</h1><p>Matched production date route: sealed Phase 5 parent versus opt-in Phase 6 construction deferral. Calendar code stays eager; the grid is constructed at first opening and retained for repeat use.</p><nav><a href="#final-timing">Cold comparisons</a><a href="#warm">Warm comparisons</a><a href="#confirmation">Firefox confirmation</a><a href="#retention">Retention</a><a href="#history">Earlier phases</a><a href="http://127.0.0.1:4240/?progress-report">Manual date review</a></nav></header>
 <section><h2>What changed</h2><p>Initial live DOM: <strong>{fmt(nodes['parentMedian'])} → {fmt(nodes['median'])} nodes ({fmt(nodes['percent'])}%)</strong>. Initial compressed JS bodies: {fmt(byte['parentMedian'])} → {fmt(byte['median'])} bytes, an added {fmt(byte['change'])} bytes.</p><p>Constrained Chromium, scoped mode: first focus <strong>{fmt(first['parentMedian'])} → {fmt(first['median'])} ms</strong>; repeat focus {fmt(repeat['parentMedian'])} → {fmt(repeat['median'])} ms. Essential shell readiness {fmt(ready['parentMedian'])} → {fmt(ready['median'])} ms. These are lab measurements of this date route, not command-palette or field INP results.</p><p>{timing:,} successful timing samples, with at least 30 per configuration, plus {retentionCount} separate 100-cycle retention runs. Cold, warm and any independent confirmation distributions remain separate. All three engines and explicit global/scoped-requested paths are covered by the primary campaign; Firefox’s scoped request uses the global fallback and is labeled accordingly.</p><p>A preceding closeout campaign completed 960 timing samples and 20 retention runs but was not promoted: WebKit reported a +4,103-byte increase, 7 bytes over the unchanged cap. Its complete distributions and source are retained separately in the new archive. Developer-facing error text was shortened; loading/retry messages and focus behavior were unchanged. The final candidate was fully requalified and remeasured.</p><p>The default remains eager, and range remains eager. Eager consumers also acquire approximately 4 KB of additional compressed code; preserving behavior is not a claim of zero payload overhead. Optional chunk loading is available explicitly but is not the recommended immediate-use policy: the earlier exploratory study found substantial constrained-network first-use costs. Its results preceded the compact loader and are not pooled into these final distributions.</p><p><strong>Acceptance:</strong> automated focus, form, lifecycle and deferred SSR checks passed, including repeated retry/localization and re-enable coverage. The unchanged practical budgets pass. Targeted VoiceOver review was accepted in Chrome, Firefox and Safari. Counted, localized failure messages resolve the reviewed Safari retry case. Original full weekday/date names remain: repeated column/date weekday speech in Chrome/Firefox is accepted to preserve WebKit context. Firefox’s functional native date button remains visible. Physical mobile and date-specific IME are not claimed. No universal focus delay, native-picker substitution, detached cache, or discard/recreate policy was introduced.</p></section>'''
