 main+='''<section id="history"><h2>Earlier phases and workload boundaries</h2><p><a href="http://127.0.0.1:4206/comparison-11f2c5584bb8.html?progress-report#final-timing">Phase 0–5 historical comparisons</a> preserve the earlier settings/command-palette campaigns, including the final Phase 5 readiness policy. Those workloads cannot be subtracted from this date-picker route. Here “Phase 5 parent” means the exact sealed parent code rebuilt into the same date consumer as Phase 6.</p><p>Phase 6 adds a measured date-specific construction boundary. It does not change the Phase 5 SSR renderer or its request-isolated worker cost. Deferred date SSR omits the calendar and preserves the native input through hydration; no-JavaScript outer-form submission still requires an actually associated native control.</p></section><section><h2>Protocol and limits</h2><p>Exact parent export and enumerated candidate overlay → generated metadata → packed packages → extracted-package Vite consumer. HTTP/2, gzip level 6, fresh browser/context per cold sample, seeded randomized matched blocks. Constrained Chromium uses 4× CPU, 150 ms latency, 1.6 Mbps down and 750 Kbps up. Touch is emulated. No outlier trimming; medians, p75 and full min/max are retained. Bootstrap intervals use 3,000 matched-block resamples and are exploratory. Long-task/layout-shift observations are retained where supported; unsupported observations are null.</p><p>Host and exact browser versions are recorded in the raw campaign manifest and samples. Each cold sample starts a fresh browser/context; host power and thermal observations are recorded at campaign start. Continuous thermal telemetry is unavailable.</p><p>Definitions share module identity and each picker uses its actual render registry. The essential field and dialog stay eager. Calendar nodes are absent initially rather than held in an inert template or detached cache. After use, the calendar stays connected when closed, so the initial node saving does not persist